TypeClassifier.classify(c: NodeCandidate) -> (NodeType, Confidence)
FieldBinder.bind(c: NodeCandidate, t: NodeType) -> (Envelope, [FieldGap])
EdgeInducer.induce(nodes: [Node], set: GoalArtifactSet) -> [Edge]
BundleWriter.emit(nodes, edges, gaps) -> BundleRef            # append-only
LossReporter.report(BundleRef) -> {extractable, partial, absent} per field
```

`FieldGap` is mandatory in the return type, not optional. An extractor that cannot report
what it failed to extract reproduces SAEOS-1 §9/A5's silent-omission failure one level
lower — the probe's own measurement would become unrepeatable in production.

### §5.3 Data flow and state machine

Node lifecycle under §4.2, with the transitions this corpus can and cannot drive:

```
      ingest
        │
        ▼
   ┌──────────┐  evidence binding (§4.2: never a frequency threshold)
   │candidate │──────────────────────────────────▶┌───────────┐
   └──────────┘                                    │ canonical │
        │                                          └───────────┘
        │ no evidence binding available                 │
        │ (F5: EvidenceRef.hash absent)                 │ superseded by
        ▼                                               ▼
   ┌──────────────────────┐                       ┌────────────┐
   │ candidate (terminal) │                       │ superseded │
   └──────────────────────┘                       └────────────┘

   ┌──────────┐   falsifier observed
   │   any    │─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ▶┌──────────┐
   └──────────┘   ✗ UNREACHABLE (F4: no falsifiers)│ refuted  │
                                                    └──────────┘
```

Two of the four transitions are unreachable from this corpus:

1. **`candidate → canonical`** requires an evidence binding. F5 removes the binding
   substrate, so every ingested node terminates as `candidate`. §5.1's projection contract
   and §7's `min_confidence` withholding would then suppress the entire graph.
2. **`* → refuted`** requires an observed falsifier. F4 makes it unreachable for all 42
   goals.

A graph in which nothing can become canonical and nothing can be refuted is an archive,
not memory — which is precisely the distinction §6.1b rule 3 claims for SAKG.

